# Dialog / Modal

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A dialog is a window that opens over the page for one focused task, to show device details or to confirm something that matters. While it is open the rest of the page waits. Home Assistant opens dialogs for More info, for editing a card, for confirmations and for the date picker. A dashboard author cannot create one or style one from a card. A theme can change its surface, corners, dividers and text colours.

Keep Home Assistant’s dialog behaviour wherever you can.

This page covers:

- Dialog / Modal
  - `<dialog>`
  - `ha-dialog`
  - `ha-dialog-header`
  - `ha-adaptive-dialog`
  - `dialog-box`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A title that names the task. In Home Assistant’s plain dialog the title is text, not a heading, unless the dialog supplies one.
- An optional subtitle.
- A close button, and sometimes a back button or action buttons in the header.
- The content, which scrolls when it is long.
- A footer with the main and the secondary action.
- The dim backdrop behind it, which can close the dialog when pressed, unless the dialog stops that.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-dialog"><code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code></h3>

The browser’s own dialog element. Opened as a modal, it makes the rest of the page inert, keeps focus inside, closes with Escape and returns focus to what opened it. It has a name given by the title and a dim backdrop. Do not add the `open` attribute for a modal; open it from script. Home Assistant does not use this element for its own dialogs, and you cannot write it in a dashboard card.

<h3 id="child-ha-dialog"><code>ha-dialog</code></h3>

Home Assistant’s dialog. It is built on a Web Awesome dialog that `ha-dialog` wraps.

- **Type:** standard, or alert. Home Assistant’s confirmations and prompts use the alert type.
- **Width:** small (320 pixels), medium (580 pixels, the default), large (1024 pixels) or full.
- **Closing:** the close button closes it. Escape and pressing the backdrop close it too, unless the dialog prevents that.
- **Small screens:** at 450 pixels wide or 500 pixels tall or less a standard dialog fills the screen with square corners.
- **Motion:** with reduced motion switched on, the opening and closing animations run for no time.
- **Parts:** header, body and footer. An element marked to take focus is focused when the dialog opens.

What the Civica theme sets: `ha-dialog-surface-background` (white in light mode, `#24282B` in dark mode), `ha-dialog-border-radius` (square), the heading and content text colours, and the line under a scrolling header (`#CECECE` in light mode, `#50565A` in dark mode).

What stays as Home Assistant draws it: the backdrop colour and blur, the shadow and the width. A theme can add these variables, but the Civica theme does not set them. Dialogs sit outside the dashboard, so card-mod on a card does not reach them.

<h3 id="child-ha-dialog-header"><code>ha-dialog-header</code></h3>

The header of a dialog. It has places for a back icon, a title, a subtitle and action buttons, and can show a line along its lower edge. The subtitle can sit above or below the title. The title is plain text, not a heading, unless the dialog that uses it adds one. The footer’s buttons come from a separate footer with a main and a secondary action.

<h3 id="child-ha-adaptive-dialog"><code>ha-adaptive-dialog</code></h3>

A dialog that changes with the screen. On a wider, taller screen it is a dialog. On a screen 870 pixels wide or less, or 500 pixels tall or less, it is a bottom sheet (see [Action Bar / Bottom Sheet](action-bar-bottom-sheet.html)). The choice is made when it opens and does not change if the window is resized, unless the dialog allows that. It can hide the close button and the header.

<h3 id="child-dialog-box"><code>dialog-box</code></h3>

Home Assistant’s confirmation, alert and prompt dialog. It has a heading, a message, and buttons.

- **Alert:** one OK button.
- **Confirmation:** a cancel button and a confirm button. Pressing the backdrop does not close it.
- **Prompt:** a field, with a label, a value and a cancel and confirm button. Pressing the backdrop does not close it once something is typed.
- A destructive confirmation shows the confirm button in a warning style and focuses Cancel first.
- The title is a heading and the message is linked as its description.

Wording comes from the part of Home Assistant that opens it. A dashboard author cannot open this dialog from a card.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A dialog stops people wandering off in the middle of a task.
- Dialogs fit small screens: they fill the screen, or become a sheet.
- Confirmation guards actions that cannot be undone.
- A dialog hides the page behind it, so people lose context. Keep it to one task.
- Some of a dialog’s behaviour belongs to Home Assistant and Web Awesome. Do not promise a particular focus or keyboard behaviour. Test the ones you rely on.
- A theme cannot change the buttons inside a dialog beyond the shared colours.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

A dialog needs:

- a clear title that names the action
- focus that moves in when it opens
- a modal that keeps focus inside while the background is inert
- an accessible close or cancel action
- Escape, where cancelling is safe
- focus that returns to the control that opened it
- content that scrolls without hiding the heading or the actions

Confirm consequential actions. Name the consequence and label the main button with a verb and a noun, such as “Delete automation”, not “Are you sure?”. Keep the safe Cancel action easy to find. Do not interrupt routine changes, such as a light or an appearance setting, with a confirmation. Keep Home Assistant’s own confirmations for locks, alarms and similar controls.

Do not remove the modal’s position, focus handling or scrolling when you tone down its look. Use a subdued backdrop, and keep the dialog’s edge clear.

Success criteria that apply:

- 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap: a person can always leave a modal, for example with Cancel.
- 2.4.3 Focus Order, 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum).
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the dialog fills a narrow screen and scrolls.
- 3.3.4 Error Prevention: confirm legal, financial or hard to reverse choices.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value and 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: a name for the dialog and its parts.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): buttons are easy to press.

One task, a plain title, the consequence in words and no time limit keep the load low.

